      "level": 1,
      "content": "public Modifiers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As the name suggests, fields and methods with public access modifiers can be accessed from any class. Let's see an example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "content": "1class Student{\n2\n3    // public field \n4    public String name;\n5}\n6\n7class Main {\n8\n9    public static void main(String[] args) {\n10\n11        // create objects of Student class\n12        Student student1 = new Student();\n13   \n14        // access the public field from the Main class\n15        student1.name = \"Felix\";\n16        System.out.println(student1.name);\n17    }\n18}\n19\n20// Output: Felix"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the above example, we have used the public access modifier with the name variable. That's why we are able to assign a new value and access its value from the Main class."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "the field inside the public can be accessed from everywhere",
